  "worked_solution": "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-3)^{2}(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-9x+13 & =A(x-3)(x+1)+B(x+1)+C(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)^{2}-9(-1)+13 & =A(-1-3)(-1+1)+B(-1+1)+C(-1-3)^{2} \\\\ 2+9+13 & =16C \\\\ 24 & =16C \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=3$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)^{2}-9(3)+13 & =A(3-3)(3+1)+B(3+1)+C(3-3)^{2} \\\\ 18-27+13 & =4B \\\\ 4 & =4B \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=0$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}-9(0)+13 & =A(0-3)(0+1)+1(0+1)+\\frac{3}{2}(0-3)^{2} \\\\ 13 & =-3A+1+\\frac{27}{2} \\\\ 3A & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
